<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Row of Pascal triangle and its sum</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Use Pascal's triangle to write down the coefficients of \((a + b)^{@n@}\), in order, as a list.</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>[[if test="is(k=1)"]]<p>(b) What do the numbers in this row add up to?</p>[[else]]<p>(b) The coefficients of \((1 + 2x)^{@n@}\) are the numbers in this row, each multiplied by a power of 2. What do the coefficients of \((1 + 2x)^{@n@}\) add up to?</p>[[/if]]<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Each number in Pascal's triangle is the sum of the two numbers above it, and every row starts and ends with 1. The single 1 at the top is the row for power 0. Building down from the row for power 4:</p><p>{@rowsd@}</p><p>(a) The coefficients of \((a + b)^{@n@}\) are [{@rowd@}].</p>[[if test="is(k=1)"]]<p>(b) Their sum is \({@sm@} = 2^{@n@}\). Putting \(a = b = 1\) in \((a + b)^{@n@}\) makes every term equal to its coefficient, so the row adds up to \((1 + 1)^{@n@}\). Each row adds up to twice the row before.</p>[[else]]<p>(b) The coefficients of \((1 + 2x)^{@n@}\) are \({@wterms@}\) in turn. Putting \(x = 1\) makes every term equal to its coefficient, so they add up to \((1 + 2)^{@n@} = 3^{@n@} = {@sm@}\). (The numbers in the row alone add up to \(2^{@n@} = {@tn@}\).)</p>[[/if]]]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[validate_row(ex) := if listp(ex) and every(integerp, ev(ex,simp)) then "" else "Type a list of whole numbers in square brackets, separated by commas, for example [1,3,3,1].";
rowstr(j) := simplode(makelist(string(binomial(j,i)),i,0,j),", ");
/* Design: next row of Pascal's triangle and its sum. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 2, Chapter 5, Section A, question A2(b).
   Keep 10 distinct variants: each power n = 6 to 10 appears with k = 1 (part (b) is the row sum, 2^n)
   and k = 2 (part (b) is the sum of the coefficients of (1 + 2x)^n, which is (1 + 2)^n: the x = 1 check of lesson 1.2). */
L: [[6,1],[9,2],[7,1],[10,2],[8,1],[6,2],[10,1],[7,2],[9,1],[8,2]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
n: L[ix][1];
k: L[ix][2];
rown: makelist(binomial(n,j),j,0,n);
prv: makelist(binomial(n-1,j),j,0,n-1);
nxt: makelist(binomial(n+1,j),j,0,n+1);
sm: (1+k)^n;
smp: (1+k)^(n-1);
smn: (1+k)^(n+1);
cdiag: if k=1 then 2^n-2 else 2^n;
rowd: rowstr(n);
rowsd: simplode(makelist(sconcat("Power ",j,": ",rowstr(j)),j,4,n),"<br/>");
rest1: rest(rown);
srest: apply("+", makelist(rest1[j]*k^(j-1), j, 1, length(rest1)));
asym: makelist(if j=1 then n+1 else binomial(n,j),j,0,n);
bad: makelist(binomial(n,j)+(if abs(2*j-n)<=1 then 1 else 0),j,0,n);
nm1: n-1; np1: n+1; tn: 2^n;
wterms: simplode(makelist(sconcat(binomial(n,j), if j=0 then "" else if j=1 then " \\times 2" else sconcat(" \\times 2^{",j,"}")),j,0,n),", ");]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
